```cpp
QDataStream &operator>>(QDataStream &s, quint8 &i)
{
    i = 0;
    s.device()->getChar((char *)&i);
    return s;
}
```

It sets `i = 0` first, then reads. So if the read fails, `i` is 0, not uninitialized.

For `ushort`:
```cpp
QDataStream &operator>>(QDataStream &s, quint16 &i)
{
    i = 0;
    s.readRawData((char *)&i, 2);
    if (s.byteOrder() == QDataStream::BigEndian)
        i = qbswap(i);
    return s;
}
```

It also sets `i = 0` first. So the fields are initialized to 0 even if the read fails.

So the header fields are always initialized. The uninitialized read must be from the image buffer.

